"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[42501],{742501:e=>{e.exports='# GPU Training on Databricks AI Runtime\n\nThis skill provides guidance for GPU programming and distributed training on Databricks AI Runtime.\n\n## OpenSSL FIPS error with opencv-python\n\nIf you encounter `OpenSSL internal error: FATAL FIPS SELFTEST FAILURE`, pin `opencv-python-headless` to version 4.12.0.88:\n\n```\n%pip install opencv-python-headless==4.12.0.88\n```\n\n## GPU health check\n\nUse the cuda health check Python library for Databricks\n\n1. Cell 1: Install the package. Allow installing this package from a third party.\n\n```\n%pip install git+https://github.com/TavnerJC/cuda-healthcheck-on-databricks.git\n```\n\nYou\'ll see a red note: "Note: you may need to restart the kernel..." - This is NORMAL!\n\n2. Cell 2: Restart Python (REQUIRED!)\n\n```python\ndbutils.library.restartPython()\n```\n\nNotebook will pause for ~10 seconds while Python restarts.\nAfter restart, the package is ready to use!\n\n3. Cell 3: Now you can import and use\n\n```python\nfrom cuda_healthcheck import run_complete_healthcheck\nimport json\n\nresult = run_complete_healthcheck()\nprint(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n```\n\n## GPU memory check and debug\n\nReport the GPU memory\n\n```python\ndef torch_mem(tag=""):\n    import torch\n    if not torch.cuda.is_available():\n        print("CUDA not available")\n        return\n    torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    alloc = torch.cuda.memory_allocated() / (1024**3)\n    resv  = torch.cuda.memory_reserved()  / (1024**3)\n    mx    = torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / (1024**3)\n    print(f"[{tag}] alloc={alloc:.2f}GB reserved={resv:.2f}GB max_alloc={mx:.2f}GB")\n\n# Example\ntorch_mem("start")\n```\n\nClear GPU cache\n\n```python\ndef clear_cuda():\n    import gc\n    try:\n        import torch\n        gc.collect()\n        if torch.cuda.is_available():\n            torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n            torch.cuda.ipc_collect()\n    except Exception:\n        gc.collect()\n```\n\n## Measure timing\n\nCPU time.time() is misleading in GPU unless you synchronize. Use the following code:\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nimport time\n\n@contextmanager\ndef timed(tag="", sync_cuda=True):\n    t0 = time.time()\n    yield\n    if sync_cuda:\n        try:\n            import torch\n            if torch.cuda.is_available():\n                torch.cuda.synchronize()\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    print(f"{tag} took {time.time() - t0:.3f}s")\n\n# Example\nwith timed("forward+backward"):\n    loss = model(x).sum()\n    loss.backward()\n```\n\n## Fine-tuning LLMs with TRL\n\nUse TRL (Transformer Reinforcement Learning) to fine-tune language models on Databricks AI Runtime.\n\n### Training methods\n\n- **SFT** (Supervised Fine-Tuning) — standard instruction tuning\n- **DPO** (Direct Preference Optimization) — alignment from preference data\n- **GRPO** (Group Relative Policy Optimization) — online RL training\n\n### SFT example with LoRA\n\n```python\n%pip install trl peft\ndbutils.library.restartPython()\n```\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer, SFTConfig\n\ndataset = load_dataset("trl-lib/Capybara", split="train")\ndataset_split = dataset.train_test_split(test_size=0.1, seed=42)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model="Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B",\n    train_dataset=dataset_split["train"],\n    eval_dataset=dataset_split["test"],\n    peft_config=LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32),\n    args=SFTConfig(\n        output_dir="/tmp/my-model",\n        num_train_epochs=3,\n        eval_strategy="steps",\n        eval_steps=50,\n    )\n)\n\ntrainer.train()\n```\n\n### Sequence length\n\nTRL config classes use `max_length` (not `max_seq_length`) to control tokenized sequence length.\nDefault is 1024. Override when you need longer context or are memory constrained:\n\n```python\nSFTConfig(max_length=2048)  # longer context\nSFTConfig(max_length=512)   # save memory\n```\n\n### Common failure modes\n\n**Out of Memory (OOM)** — try in order:\n\n1. Reduce batch size: `per_device_train_batch_size=1`, increase `gradient_accumulation_steps=8`\n2. Enable gradient checkpointing: `gradient_checkpointing=True`\n3. Use LoRA/PEFT for models >7B to reduce memory\n\n**Dataset format error** — ensure format matches training method:\n\n- SFT: `messages`, text, or prompt-completion columns\n- DPO: `prompt`, `chosen`, `rejected` columns\n- GRPO: prompt-only column\n\n## Distributed training\n\n[Reference](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/ai-runtime/distributed-training)\n\nThe `@distributed` decorator from the `serverless_gpu` package lets you launch multi-GPU workloads\nwith minimal code changes. It integrates with PyTorch DDP, FSDP, and DeepSpeed.\n\n### Quick start\n\nWrap your training code in a function and apply the `@distributed` decorator. The `gpu_type`\nparameter must match the accelerator type your notebook is connected to.\n\n```python\nfrom serverless_gpu import distributed\n\n@distributed(gpus=8, gpu_type=\'H100\')\ndef run_train():\n    ...\n\nrun_train.distributed()\n```\n\n### Supported frameworks\n\n- **PyTorch DDP** (Distributed Data Parallel) — data parallelism across GPUs\n- **FSDP** (Fully Sharded Data Parallel) — shards model parameters, gradients, and optimizer states\n- **DeepSpeed** — memory-efficient training with ZeRO optimization stages\n\n### Dataloading\n\nMove data loading code inside the `@distributed` function to avoid pickle serialization errors\nwith large datasets. Use Unity Catalog Volumes for data access.\n\n### Rendezvous in multiple GPU processes\n\nCall the torch distributed initialize process group if the custom library doesn\'t have the\nrendezvous step. Always set the CUDA device **before** initializing the process group so that\neach rank\'s NCCL communicator binds to the correct GPU.\n\n```python\ndef setup():\n    local_rank = int(os.environ["LOCAL_RANK"])\n    torch.cuda.set_device(local_rank)\n    dist.init_process_group("nccl")\n\ndef cleanup():\n    dist.destroy_process_group()\n```\n\n### Debugging methodology\n\n#### Evidence standards\n\n**Verify every factual claim against the logs.** Grep for exact error messages, metadata fields,\nand URLs before citing them. Misattributing failures is worse than saying "unable to determine\nroot cause."\n\n- **Numeric claims** (e.g., "22 truncation warnings") must be grep-counted exactly. Never estimate.\n- **Line numbers**: Use the traceback\'s line numbers, not source code (the deployed version may\n  differ).\n- **Wall time**: Calculate from log timestamps, not run metadata (which includes queuing,\n  provisioning, and zombie periods). If logs lack timestamps, say so and cite metadata times\n  with a caveat.\n- **Inferences**: Mark extrapolations explicitly as "inferred" or "unverifiable."\n- **Configured vs. observed values**: Never substitute configuration values (e.g., `NUM_NODES`,\n  `WORLD_SIZE`, requested instance types) for observed system state (e.g., actual GPU memory,\n  actual node count). Report what logs show.\n- **No speculative hardware diagnoses**: Do not default to OOM, instance type mismatch, or GPU\n  compatibility hypotheses without log evidence. Many failures have mundane causes (missing\n  directories, typos, dependency conflicts). If logs are unavailable, state the root cause is\n  indeterminate.\n- **Security**: Redact credentials, tokens, and API keys in reports (e.g., `DATABRICKS_TOKEN`,\n  bearer tokens).\n\n#### Compound and phased failures\n\n- Enumerate **all** distinct errors, not just the first. A run may fail from both a missing\n  dependency and an invalid config.\n- For phased pipelines (download, process, train, upload), verify which phase failed via\n  completion markers. Do not attribute failure to a completed phase.\n\n#### Solutions\n\nProvide solutions ranked by confidence:\n\n- **High (90%+)**: Direct fixes for clear errors\n- **Medium (50-90%)**: Likely solutions based on patterns\n- **Low (below 50%)**: Exploratory suggestions when root cause is unclear\n\nEvery recommendation must be grounded in evidence from logs or configuration.\nAcknowledge uncertainty explicitly. Ask clarifying questions when logs are ambiguous or incomplete.\n\n### Troubleshooting distributed training\n\nNote: the below guidance only applies to code running pytorch.\n\n#### NCCL errors\n\nNCCL (NVIDIA Collective Communications Library) errors are the most common failure in multi-GPU\nand multi-node training. Enable verbose logging to diagnose:\n\n```python\nimport os\nos.environ["NCCL_DEBUG"] = "INFO"\nos.environ["NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS"] = "ALL"\n```\n\nSet these **before** `dist.init_process_group()` or the `@distributed` call. The output shows\ntransport selection (NVLink, IB, TCP), ring topology, and the exact collective operation that\nfailed.\n\n#### Multi-node and NCCL failure analysis\n\nIn multi-node runs, NCCL timeouts are almost always **symptoms** of another rank failing first.\nCheck all ranks and use timestamps to reconstruct event order.\n\nRead the full stack trace to determine **where** the NCCL timeout occurred.\n\nAn error citing a single rank (e.g., "rank 6") often means all ranks hit the same issue. Verify\nby checking other ranks before claiming the failure was isolated.\n\nWhen citing NCCL errors, distinguish **NCCL WARN messages** (peer IPs, socket operations like\n`Connection reset by peer`) from the **Python exception\'s `Last error` field** (e.g.,\n`Error encountered progressing operation=Connect`). These are different layers reporting the same\nfailure.\n\n#### DistStoreError and DistBackendError\n\nThese PyTorch `c10d` errors indicate failures in the distributed store (used for coordination)\nor the communication backend (NCCL):\n\n- **`c10::DistStoreError`** — the TCP store used for rendezvous lost connection. Common when a\n  worker node dies or becomes unreachable. Check that training code doesn\'t crash on a subset of workers.\n- **`c10::DistBackendError`** — wraps an underlying NCCL failure. Read the nested error message\n  for the root cause (usually NCCL timeout or hardware fault). A common example:\n\n  ```\n  NCCL/collective error detected: DistBackendError\n  ncclSystemError: System call (e.g. socket, malloc) or external library call failed or device error.\n  Last error:socketPollConnect poll() returned 1, no POLLOUT events. Force-exiting.\n  ```\n\n  **Diagnosis:** Check if `torch.cuda.set_device(local_rank)` is called **before**\n  `dist.init_process_group("nccl")`. When NCCL initializes, it creates communication contexts on\n  the currently active CUDA device. If the device hasn\'t been set yet, every rank may default to\n  `cuda:0`, which causes contention (or outright errors on multi-GPU nodes). Setting the device\n  first ensures each rank\'s NCCL communicator binds to the correct GPU.\n\n#### Timeout configuration\n\nThe default NCCL timeout (30 minutes) can be too short for large-scale jobs where some collective\noperations take longer (e.g., large all-reduce at 64+ GPUs). Increase it:\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nimport torch.distributed as dist\n\ndist.init_process_group(\n    "nccl",\n    timeout=datetime.timedelta(minutes=60),\n)\n```\n\n#### Slow distributed training\n\nIf multi-node training is significantly slower than expected:\n\n1. **Check NCCL transport**: In `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` output, look for `Using network` lines. Training\n   over TCP/IP is much slower than InfiniBand or NVLink. On H100 SGC, you should see\n   high-bandwidth transports.\n2. **Profile communication overhead**: Use PyTorch profiler to identify whether time is spent in\n   compute or communication:\n\n   ```python\n   from torch.profiler import profile, ProfilerActivity\n\n   with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA]) as prof:\n       # run one training step\n       pass\n   print(prof.key_averages().table(sort_by="cuda_time_total", row_limit=20))\n   ```\n\n3. **Verify data loading**: Slow I/O can stall all GPUs. Use Unity Catalog Volumes and ensure\n   data is in the same region as the compute.\n\n### Common failure patterns\n\n#### GPU out of memory (OOM)\n\n- **Batch 1 crash** = model doesn\'t fit in GPU memory. Requires a config change (smaller batch\n  size, LoRA/PEFT, gradient checkpointing, or FSDP sharding) — retrying won\'t help.\n- **Batch N crash** = memory leak or accumulation. Check for tensors not being freed, growing\n  activation caches, or logging callbacks that retain references.\n\n#### System OOM (exit 137 / SIGKILL)\n\nThe kernel killed the process for exceeding system (CPU) RAM. Common during large data downloads,\ntokenization of big datasets, or when too many workers load data simultaneously. Reduce\n`dataloader_num_workers` or stream data instead of loading it all at once.\n\n#### External termination (exit 143 / SIGTERM)\n\nThe process was killed externally (e.g., job timeout, preemption, or SGC workload manager). The\nlast log entry shows what **completed**, not what was interrupted. If the last line is "Checkpoint\nsaved," the process was killed _after_ that operation — do not assume checkpointing caused the\nfailure.\n\n#### HuggingFace gated model / API rate limits (429)\n\nWhen multiple ranks download a gated model simultaneously, HuggingFace may throttle requests.\nDownload the model to a Unity Catalog Volume first, then load from the local path:\n\n```python\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("/Volumes/catalog/schema/models/my-model")\n```\n\n#### Checkpoint save/load failures\n\n- **Save failures**: Check disk space in the output directory. For Unity Catalog Volumes, verify\n  write permissions. Large models may need `save_safetensors=True` for more reliable serialization.\n- **Load failures**: Ensure the checkpoint was fully written (partial saves from crashes are\n  corrupt). Verify the model architecture matches the checkpoint.\n\n#### Cleanup ordering in multi-node runs\n\nWhen one node initiates cleanup (e.g., calling `ray stop --force`), it can kill shared workers\nbefore other nodes reach a synchronization barrier. The other nodes then time out at\n`dist.barrier()` with a gloo timeout error. The barrier timeout is a **symptom** — trace which\nnode initiated shutdown first.\n\n#### Attention mask shape mismatches (1D vs 2D)\n\nWhen training or fine-tuning models, attention mask shape errors (`RuntimeError: The size of\ntensor a (X) must match the size of tensor b (Y)`) can occur when one component produces a 1D\nmask and another expects 2D (or vice versa). Verify which component produces the mask and which\nconsumes it. Fix the producer\'s `output_attention_mask` config or add a reshape.\n\n#### Import and dependency failures\n\nDistinguish build-time failures (`pip install -e .` invoking setup.py) from runtime failures\n(`import torch`). Build-time failures require the dependency in the base image or environment\nsetup. Runtime import errors mean the package installed but isn\'t importable (version conflicts,\nmissing native libraries, etc.).\n\n#### Empty/None config values\n\nA YAML or JSON config key with all entries commented out or set to `null` resolves to `None`,\nnot `[]`. Code calling `len()` or iterating on it crashes with\n`TypeError: object of type \'NoneType\' has no len()`.\n'}}]);
